## 11.4 UI Rules

Use a consistent application shell with sidebar/top navigation, page title, breadcrumbs where useful, content area and user menu.

Use loading, empty, success and error states for asynchronous screens.

Use confirmation dialogs for destructive actions.

Do not display controls that the current role cannot use.

Do not rely on hidden buttons as a security mechanism; backend authorization remains mandatory.

Use accessible labels and keyboard-friendly interactive components.

Use pagination or infinite loading for large student/submission lists.

Provide search/filter/sort where lists can become large.

# 12. Security Design

Authentication is required for all private academic data.

Authorization must be enforced server-side for every resource.

Students can access only their own private academic records and courses in which they are enrolled.

Lecturers can access academic data only for courses assigned to them.

Use least privilege for database/storage access.

Never expose service-role credentials or database secrets to frontend code.

Use HTTPS in production.

Validate uploaded file MIME type/extension and size; do not trust filename alone.

Store files in private buckets when they contain student submissions or otherwise sensitive data; issue authorized/signed access as appropriate.

Protect against IDOR by checking ownership/membership before returning a resource.

Protect against injection using parameterized queries/ORM/query builders and validated inputs.

Use CSRF protection if cookie-based authentication is used in a cross-site configuration; otherwise follow the chosen token architecture correctly.

Implement rate limiting or abuse protection on authentication and high-risk endpoints where practical.

Log security-relevant events without logging passwords, tokens or sensitive secrets.

Keep dependencies updated and scan for known vulnerabilities.

Use environment variables for secrets and provide .env.example without real credentials.

# 13. Validation and Business Rules

| **Area** | **Rules** |
| --- | --- |
| Email | Must be syntactically valid; normalize according to authentication provider policy. |
| Password | Must meet authentication provider security policy; never return it in API responses. |
| Course Code | Required and unique according to course-offering policy. |
| Assignment | Title and due date required; max_marks > 0; due date must follow configured rules. |
| Submission | Only authorized enrolled student; accepted file types and maximum size enforced; deadline policy enforced. |
| Marks | 0 <= mark <= max_marks; only authorized lecturer may enter/edit. |
| Attendance | Only enrolled students receive records; valid status values only. |
| Announcement | Title/body required; publication state controls visibility. |
| Material | Title/file required; file type and size restrictions enforced. |
| Profile | Users may update only fields permitted by role and security policy. |

# 14. File and Storage Design

Use separate logical storage areas/buckets for learning materials and student submissions. Database records store metadata and storage paths; the actual binary files remain in object storage.

Example storage layout:
materials/
  {course_id}/{material_id}/{filename}

submissions/
  {course_id}/{assignment_id}/{student_id}/{submission_id}/{filename}

Rules:
- Do not expose private bucket URLs permanently.
- Verify user authorization before issuing a download/access URL.
- Sanitize filenames and never use user-provided paths directly.
- Enforce maximum file size.
- Restrict file extensions/MIME types to the supported list.
- Consider virus/malware scanning before production use.

Suggested initial allowed types: PDF, DOCX, PPTX, XLSX, CSV, PNG, JPG/JPEG, ZIP, subject to institutional requirements. The implementation should make this list configurable rather than hard-coded throughout the frontend.

# 15. Audit and Data Integrity

Record important changes to marks, attendance corrections, assignment grading and other sensitive academic records.

Audit records should contain actor, action, entity type, entity ID, timestamp and relevant before/after values where appropriate.

Do not allow ordinary users to edit audit history.

Use database constraints and transactions for academic operations requiring consistency.

Avoid destructive deletion of academic history when a soft-delete/archive state is more appropriate.

# 16. Error Handling

| **Situation** | **Expected UI/API Behavior** |
| --- | --- |
| Unauthenticated | Redirect/return 401 and ask user to authenticate. |
| Unauthorized resource | Return 403 or 404 according to security policy; do not leak existence of protected resources unnecessarily. |
| Not found | Return 404 with user-friendly message. |
| Validation failure | Return structured field-level errors. |
| Duplicate record | Return 409 conflict with actionable message. |
| File too large/invalid type | Reject upload with clear allowed limits/types. |
| Network/server error | Show retry-friendly message; log technical detail server-side. |
| Expired session | Prompt user to sign in again and avoid losing unsaved work where possible. |

# 17. Testing Strategy

## 17.1 Test Levels

Unit tests: validation, calculations, business services and utility functions.

Integration tests: API + database interactions, authorization and workflows.

Frontend component tests: forms, tables, dialogs, loading/error states.

End-to-end tests: critical student and lecturer journeys.

Security tests: role isolation, IDOR attempts, unauthorized file access and input validation.

Performance smoke tests: dashboard/list endpoints and common queries.

## 17.2 Critical Acceptance Tests

| **ID** | **Acceptance Test** |
| --- | --- |
| AT-01 | Student can log in and reach student dashboard. |
| AT-02 | Lecturer can log in and reach lecturer dashboard. |
| AT-03 | Student cannot access lecturer-only endpoint. |
| AT-04 | Lecturer cannot access a course that is not assigned to them. |
| AT-05 | Student cannot view another student's marks. |
| AT-06 | Student cannot submit to a course they are not enrolled in. |
| AT-07 | Lecturer can create an assignment for an assigned course. |
| AT-08 | Student can submit a valid assignment before deadline. |
| AT-09 | Invalid/oversized submission is rejected. |
| AT-10 | Lecturer can grade submission with valid mark. |
| AT-11 | Mark above maximum is rejected. |
| AT-12 | Student sees released mark but not unpublished mark. |
| AT-13 | Lecturer can create attendance session and mark students. |
| AT-14 | Student can view attendance percentage. |
| AT-15 | Student can download authorized course material. |
| AT-16 | Student cannot access another course's private material. |
| AT-17 | Lecturer can publish announcement and enrolled students can see it. |
| AT-18 | Important events generate notifications. |
| AT-19 | Duplicate enrollment/attendance/assessment mark records are prevented. |
| AT-20 | Core reports show consistent totals with source records. |

# 18. Deployment and Environment Design

Development:
- React/Vite frontend
- Local FastAPI backend
- Supabase project/environment
- .env files excluded from Git

Production:
User Browser
   -> Frontend Hosting (HTTPS)
   -> FastAPI API (HTTPS)
   -> Supabase Auth/PostgreSQL/Storage

Recommended environment separation:
- Development
- Production

Never commit real secrets, service-role keys, database passwords, or production credentials.

## 18.1 Environment Variables

Frontend example:
VITE_API_BASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

Backend example:
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=        # backend only; never frontend
DATABASE_URL=                     # if direct DB access is used
CORS_ORIGINS=
ENVIRONMENT=

Use the minimum required credentials for each component. If Supabase Auth is used directly for authentication, ensure the FastAPI backend verifies the authenticated identity and does not blindly trust client-supplied user IDs.

# 19. Git, CI/CD and Development Workflow

Use Git with feature branches for non-trivial changes.

Protect the main branch.

Require tests/lint checks before merging when possible.

Use pull requests for reviewed changes even when working alone, if practical, to preserve engineering discipline.

Run frontend lint/type/test checks and backend tests in CI.

Build the frontend in CI to catch production build failures.

Do not deploy if critical tests fail.

Keep database/schema changes versioned and reproducible.

Document deployment steps in README.md.

## 19.1 Suggested Commit Style

feat: add assignment creation
fix: prevent marks above maximum
test: add attendance authorization tests
refactor: separate course service
docs: update API specification

# 20. Development Roadmap

| **Phase** | **Name** | **Deliverables** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Phase 0 | Project foundation | Repository, frontend/backend scaffolding, environment setup, coding standards, CI basics |
| Phase 1 | Authentication & profiles | Supabase Auth, role resolution, protected routes, profile data |
| Phase 2 | Courses & enrollment | Course model, lecturer assignment, student enrollment, course pages |
| Phase 3 | Materials | Storage integration, upload/download, access control |
| Phase 4 | Assignments | Assignment CRUD, student submission, file handling, deadlines |
| Phase 5 | Grading & marks | Assessments, marks, release rules, calculations |
| Phase 6 | Attendance | Sessions, records, calculations, reports |
| Phase 7 | Announcements & notifications | Course announcements, in-app notifications |
| Phase 8 | Dashboards & reports | Role dashboards, filters, reporting views |
| Phase 9 | Hardening | Security review, tests, validation, performance, accessibility |
| Phase 10 | Deployment & documentation | Production deployment, README, API docs, user guide, final demonstration |

# 21. Overall Definition of Done

Both roles can authenticate securely.

Role-based frontend routing and backend authorization work correctly.

Students can access only their own and their enrolled-course data.

Lecturers can access only their assigned-course data.

Courses and enrollments are functional.

Materials can be securely uploaded and accessed.

Assignments can be created, published, submitted and graded.

Attendance can be recorded and summarized.

Marks can be entered, validated, calculated and released.

Announcements and notifications function.

Dashboards display real data rather than hard-coded placeholders.

Critical API and UI tests pass.

No known critical security issue remains.

Production environment variables and deployment instructions are documented.

Database schema and API documentation are synchronized with implementation.

The system has seed/demo data for final presentation without exposing real student information.

# 22. Instructions for an AI Coding Assistant

This section is intended to be copied/read by an AI coding assistant. The assistant must treat the SRS as the authoritative project specification.

Do not add an Administrator role unless the project owner explicitly requests it.

Do not add major features outside Version 1.0 scope without explicit approval.

Implement the project incrementally by phase; do not generate the entire application in one uncontrolled change.

Before coding a feature, identify its requirements, database entities, API endpoints, authorization rules, frontend pages, validation and tests.

Never put secrets in source code.

Never rely only on frontend checks for authorization.

Every protected resource must enforce server-side authorization.

Use the database as the source of truth for persistent academic data.

Do not hard-code dashboard statistics or academic records.

Use reusable components and services instead of duplicated logic.

Keep API schemas, database models and frontend types consistent.

Add tests for each important business rule and authorization boundary.

When a requirement is ambiguous, choose the simplest implementation consistent with this document and flag the ambiguity instead of inventing a large feature.

Do not silently change database structure or API contracts; document and explain migrations/contract changes.

Do not delete academic records merely to simplify implementation.

Maintain clear error handling and loading/empty states.

After each phase, run tests and verify the acceptance criteria before proceeding.

Keep the application usable without AI features; AI is not a required dependency for Version 1.0.

# 23. Risks and Mitigation

| **Risk** | **Impact** | **Mitigation** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Scope creep | High | Use this SRS as baseline; implement phases in order. |
| Weak authorization | High | Centralize backend authorization and test role boundaries. |
| File storage/security issues | High | Use private storage, validation and signed/authorized access. |
| Inconsistent marks | High | Use validation, constraints, transactions and audit logs. |
| Large datasets | Medium | Pagination, indexing and filtered queries. |
| Lost work during deployment | High | Backups/versioned migrations and separate environments. |
| Overengineering | Medium | Prefer simple architecture and only required features. |
| AI coding assistant changes scope | Medium | Keep SRS in repository and explicitly require compliance. |

# 24. Assumptions

The project is initially for one institution or academic environment.

Only Student and Lecturer roles are application users in Version 1.0.

Initial account/course/enrollment provisioning may be performed through controlled setup/seed data or a future administration process.

The project has reliable internet access for normal operation.

Supabase is acceptable as the managed PostgreSQL/Auth/Storage platform.

The institution's exact grading scale and attendance policy can be configured later without redesigning the core architecture.

The application is not intended to replace the institution's official student information system unless integration is explicitly added.

# 25. Pre-Coding Checklist

Confirm project title and problem statement with supervisor.

Confirm that Student and Lecturer are the only Version 1.0 roles.

Confirm grading and attendance rules with the academic context.

Confirm supported file types and size limits.

Confirm whether timetable data is manually seeded or entered through the lecturer workflow.

Create Supabase project and separate development/production environments if possible.

Create Git repository and initial README.

Create database migration/schema plan.

Create API contract/OpenAPI baseline.

Create frontend route map.

Create initial seed/demo dataset.

Begin Phase 0 only after the above decisions are recorded.

